<Step enable="True" id="89" name="# (comment)"><DisableStepCollapsed state="False"/><Restore state="False"/><Text>__kp_CustomerEquipment is assigned by the server. The offline file's own</Text></Step>
<Step enable="True" id="89" name="# (comment)"><DisableStepCollapsed state="False"/><Restore state="False"/><Text>serial fires on New Record, but its range came with the copy of</Text></Step>
<Step enable="True" id="89" name="# (comment)"><DisableStepCollapsed state="False"/><Restore state="False"/><Text>production this file was cloned from -- a positive local key would sit in</Text></Step>
<Step enable="True" id="89" name="# (comment)"><DisableStepCollapsed state="False"/><Restore state="False"/><Text>the server's upcoming range. Overwrite it with a negative key, as</Text></Step>
<Step enable="True" id="89" name="# (comment)"><DisableStepCollapsed state="False"/><Restore state="False"/><Text>SaveLocation does: never a collision, and negative reads as 'not uploaded</Text></Step>
<Step enable="True" id="89" name="# (comment)"><DisableStepCollapsed state="False"/><Restore state="False"/><Text>yet'. UploadChanges writes the real key back after the POST.</Text></Step>
<Step enable="True" id="141" name="Set Variable"><DisableStepCollapsed state="False"/><Value><Calculation><![CDATA[Let ( m = GetAsNumber ( ExecuteSQL ( "select min ( \"__kp_CustomerEquipment\" ) from \"CUSTOMER_EQUIPMENT\" where \"__kp_CustomerEquipment\" < 0" ; "" ; "" ) ) ; If ( m < 0 ; m - 1 ; -1 ) )]]></Calculation></Value><Repetition><Calculation><![CDATA[1]]></Calculation></Repetition><Name>$localKey</Name></Step>
